# Translations
*.mo
*.pot

# ...EXCEPT the ones that are build output and have to ship: the dashboard's
# (#97) and, since #231, the bot's.
#
# The stock Python ignore above is right for a project that compiles its
# catalogues at build or install time. This one cannot: Dockerfile-dashboard
# copies src/dashboard/ and installs no toolchain, because Babel is a dev
# dependency and requirements-dashboard.txt argues that every package on the
# internet-facing host is something an attacker gets to probe.
#
# So the .mo files are the artefact that gets deployed, and an ignored .mo is
# a dashboard that serves English in every language while every .po in the
# tree says otherwise -- with nothing to indicate it, because gettext falls
# back silently. tests/test_i18n.py asserts each one is present for exactly
# that reason.
#
# The .pot rides along so `pybabel update` has a baseline in a fresh clone
# rather than regenerating one and marking the whole catalogue changed.
!src/dashboard/translations/**/*.mo
!src/dashboard/translations/*.pot

# Same argument, same shape, for the bot's catalogues (#231). Dockerfile-bot
# copies src/ and installs no toolchain either, and the bot's failure mode is
# worse than the dashboard's: these strings arrive in a DM, unprompted, to
# somebody who may never open the website at all.
!src/translations/bot/**/*.mo
!src/translations/bot/*.pot

